<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Delhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">inaugurated <a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, a planned city designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens">Edwin Lutyens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-baker">Herbert Baker</a> south of old Delhi. The move of the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> was announced at the Delhi Durbar of 1911, and the new city was inaugurated in 1931. After independence in 1947 it remained the seat of the government of the Republic of India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-delhi.jpg" alt="New Delhi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ronakshah1990 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Forecourt,_Rashtrapati_Bhavan_-_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished among the new middle classes: Rabindranath Tagore won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913, and the architecture of <b>New Delhi</b>, designed by Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker and inaugurated in 1931, still houses the government of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>India hosted the G20 summit in <b>New Delhi</b> in September 2023.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Bahá’í Houses of Worship, open to people of all faiths, have nine sides and a central dome, and their services consist of readings from the scriptures of all religions, without sermons; among the best known is the Lotus Temple in <b>New Delhi</b>, completed in 1986.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The movement’s seventh summit was to meet in Baghdad in 1982, during the Iran–Iraq War; after Iranian aircraft bombed Baghdad’s Al-Dura refinery to show that its airspace was unsafe, the summit was held in <b>New Delhi</b> in March 1983.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Miniature paintings of the Gita Govinda are held in museums from Bhubaneswar and <b>New Delhi</b> to New York, Zürich and Paris.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gita-govinda">Gita Govinda</a></li><li><blockquote>Jacqueline Hirst and John Zavos record it being sung in places as far apart as <b>New Delhi</b>, Toledo and Leicester.</blockquote><a href="/songs/om-jai-jagdish-hare">Om Jai Jagdish Hare</a></li></ul></section>
